/// Limit x to a range.
///
/// The value of `x` brought into the range from `low` to `high`: `low` where `x` is below it,
/// `high` where `x` is above it, and `x` itself in between. It is `min(max(x, low), high)`, so an
/// Int stays an Int when all three are Ints, `NA` in any argument gives `NA`, and when `low` is
/// above `high` the result is `high`, as R's `pmin(pmax(x, low), high)` gives. Given a column, it
/// applies to every row in one pass.
///
/// x: The value, or a column of values.
/// low: The smallest value the result may have.
/// high: The largest value the result may have.
/// returns: `x` within the range, a Float, or an Int when all three are Ints; a column when `x` is one.
/// missing values: `NA` in any of the three gives `NA`. In a column each row is taken by itself: a row with `NA` stays `NA`, no row is dropped and nothing is printed for it.
/// details: Exact: the result is one of its three arguments, and nothing is rounded.
/// reference: The definition is the two comparisons `min(max(x, low), high)`; nothing is estimated.
/// coming from: clamp(x, low, high) | pmin(pmax(x, low), high) | clip(x, low, high)
/// coming from: R has no single function. Stata's `clip` returns missing when `low` is above `high`, where Zeff and the R form return `high`.
/// related: To cap from one side only, use `min` or `max`.
/// see also: min, max, between
/// example: Above the range, the upper limit.
/// > clamp(5, 0, 3)
/// 3
/// example: Below the range, the lower limit; a Float among the three gives a Float.
/// > clamp(-1.5, 0, 3)
/// 0
/// example: Inside the range, the value itself.
/// > clamp(1.5, 0, 3)
/// 1.5
/// example: Row by row on the shipped wages: one worker's hours held between 30 and 40.
/// > let w = dataset(wages).where(worker == 19).select([worker, year, wage, hours, exper])
/// > w.add(held = clamp(hours, 30, 40))
/// frame · 5 rows × 6 variables · panel worker × year
///
///       worker   year    wage   hours   exper   held
///   1       19   2012   12.64    29.2       0     30
///   2       19   2013   25.18    31.4       0   31.4
///   3       19   2014   35.49    33.5       1   33.5
///   4       19   2015   26.07    58.2       2     40
///   5       19   2016      NA      30       3     30
fn clamp(x: Float, low: Float, high: Float) -> Float {
    min(max(x, low), high)
}
